\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For a countable discrete group G, does amenability follow from this property: for every $\varepsilon >0$ there exists $\delta >0$ such that, on every complex Hilbert space H, each normalized map $\mu :G\to U(H)$ with $sup_{g,h}\Vert \mu (gh)-\mu (g)\mu (h)\Vert \leq \delta $ is within $\varepsilon $ uniformly on G of a genuine unitary representation on that same H?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For a countable discrete group G, does amenability follow from this property: for every $\varepsilon >0$ there exists $\delta >0$ such that, on every complex Hilbert space H, each normalized map $\mu :G\to U(H)$ with $sup_{g,h}\Vert \mu (gh)-\mu (g)\mu (h)\Vert \leq \delta $ is within $\varepsilon $ uniformly on G of a genuine unitary representation on that same H?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-012-0050-z}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Strong-Ulam-Stability-Characterizes-Amenability-October-5-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

