\section*{Strong Ulam stability characterizes amenability}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. For countable discrete groups, uniform stability of all unitary almost-representations is claimed equivalent to amenability, allowing infinite-dimensional spaces.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Boundary actions supply finite flags and frame configurations. Metric-transfer and block-operator comparisons construct arbitrarily accurate unitary defects that remain a definite distance from exact representations when amenability fails; the amenable direction uses the established stability theorem.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The paper defines strong Ulam stability with normalized unitary maps, the uniform operator-norm defect and all complex Hilbert spaces, and proves amenability iff this property for every countable group. lean/docs/251.md documents the different unitarizability companion and does not cover this new approximate-representation theorem. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 251, the documented theorem gives amenability--unitarizability equivalence for arbitrary discrete groups and almost-unitary nonunitarizable witnesses; countable groups have separable witnesses. The later strong-Ulam theorem is not selected. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

\subsection{Sources}
